{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1315637744/3/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"332f06d7\" lines=\"9-23\">\n<description>\nSlide a solid square through a winding channel to reach a same-sized target square.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg (most common color). A river color forms a winding corridor.\n- Two same-sized NxN solid squares on the river: mover and destination, each a distinct color.\n\nRule:\n- The mover square travels along the river toward the destination.\n- It can only pass through positions where the full NxN area is river.\n- If reachable, the mover replaces the destination; otherwise it stops at the farthest reachable point.\n- The mover's original position is filled with the river color.\n\nKey insight: Chokepoints narrower than N pixels block the square, forcing it to stop short.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"68bc2e87\" lines=\"46-60\">\n<description>\nRecover the bottom-to-top stacking order of overlaid rectangular color frames.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is one color, and every foreground color is a one-cell-thick rectangular frame.\n- Overlapping frame pixels show only the color of the frame that is highest at that location.\n\nRule:\n- Reconstruct each frame as the border of the bounding box of its visible pixels.\n- At every cell covered by multiple reconstructed frames, the visible frame color is above the other frames covering that cell.\n- Return the unique bottom-to-top order of frame colors as a single-column grid.\n\nKey insight:\nThe hidden parts of each frame do not need to be seen directly; the visible pixels reveal the full frame bbox, and overlap cells reveal the layer constraints.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"9968a131\" lines=\"10-27\">\n<description>\nShift alternating two-color row bars so each row aligns to the topmost bar's color columns.\n\nInput structure:\n- The dominant background surrounds horizontal two-pixel bars.\n- Every foreground row has exactly one adjacent bar using the same two colors.\n- The topmost bar gives the reference left/right column for each color.\n\nRule:\n- Keep bars whose left color is already in its reference column.\n- For a reversed bar, shift the whole two-pixel bar horizontally until its\n  left color lands in that color's reference column.\n- Background cells vacated by shifted bars become background.\n\nKey insight:\n- The rows are not recolored; each two-color bar is translated so equal\n  colors line up with the topmost reference bar.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"f560132c\" lines=\"259-273\">\n<description>\nAssemble scattered jigsaw pieces into a rectangle, recoloring each by a legend sprite's color map.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Several monochrome non-bg sprites (the \"pieces\"), scattered across the grid.\n- One sprite is the legend: it contains a multi-color k×k patch (typically 2×2) encoding output colors.\n\nRule:\n- Tile all pieces (rotations/reflections allowed) into a tight rectangle with no gaps.\n- The legend's k×k color patch defines a spatial color map over the rectangle.\n- Each piece is recolored to the legend-map color corresponding to its position in the assembled rectangle.\n\nKey insight: The legend piece doubles as both a jigsaw piece and a color key — its internal k×k palette assigns colors by spatial quadrant.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"fe45cba4\" lines=\"72-89\">\n<description>\nMove the left-edge fragment into its same-colored anchor fragment to complete a solid rectangle.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has a dominant background color.\n- Exactly one foreground color is split into two 4-connected objects: one\n  touches the left edge, and the other is an anchor away from the left edge.\n- The translated left-edge object and the anchor can form one filled rectangle.\nRule\n- Find the unique split color whose left-edge object can be translated onto\n  background cells beside the other same-colored object so their union is a\n  solid axis-aligned rectangle.\n- Clear the left-edge object's original cells to the background.\n- Paint that object at the unique completing offset, preserving all other cells.\nKey insight\n- The active color is identified by object structure and rectangle completion,\n  not by a fixed color id; the same-colored anchor object stays in place.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"8b7bacbf\" lines=\"42-56\">\n<description>\nFill enclosed regions along a line seeded by unique border pixels.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Colored lines/drawings on the grid.\n- Unique single-pixel colors on the grid border mark seed endpoints of each line.\n\nRule:\n- Each border seed pixel connects (8-connected) to a line drawing.\n- Find closed bg cavities whose boundary touches that line.\n- Flood-fill each such cavity with the seed pixel's color.\n\nKey insight: Only cavities reachable through the seed's own line get filled; the seed's unique color acts as a per-line fill label.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"98c475bf\" lines=\"62-76\">\n<description>\nErase the complete interior glyph and expand each endpoint marker pair into its color's full glyph.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid is 20x20 with background inside two same-color full-height vertical frame bars.\n- Inside the frame there is one complete color glyph and one or more other colors appearing only as two endpoint markers in the same row.\n- Marker endpoints are always in the first and last interior columns.\n\nRule\n- Keep the vertical frame unchanged.\n- Erase any complete interior glyph.\n- For each endpoint marker pair, use that marker color's learned glyph template and anchor its horizontal bar on the marker row.\n\nKey insight\n- Color identifies which glyph shape to draw; the two endpoint pixels are not copied objects, they are anchors for reconstructing that color's full symbol.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"135a2760\" lines=\"6-19\">\n<description>\nRepair noisy periodic patterns inside frame-delimited grid regions.\n\nInput structure:\n- Frame color (most common) forms full rows/cols, dividing the grid into rectangular regions.\n- bg (second most common) fills empty space; each region contains a small colored tile repeated periodically with a few corrupted pixels.\n\nRule:\n- For each region, find the bounding box of non-bg content.\n- Infer the smallest repeating tile that explains the pattern (majority vote per residue position), requiring it repeat at least 2x per axis.\n- Replace the region with the clean tiled version.\n\nKey insight: Tile dimensions are unknown and must be inferred; prefer the smallest tile with genuine repetition over one that memorizes the entire region.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"ff2825db\" lines=\"18-36\">\n<description>\nSelect the majority interior marker color, then redraw the framed board as that color's frame plus its bounding-box outline.\n\nInput structure:\n- The top row is a copied palette made from adjacent equal color pairs.\n- Below it is a one-cell-thick rectangular frame around a black interior.\n- Non-black, non-frame interior pixels are marker colors; one marker color has\n  the unique highest count.\n\nRule:\n- Keep the palette row unchanged.\n- Pick the uniquely most frequent interior marker color.\n- Clear the lower board to black, recolor its outer frame with the picked color,\n  and draw the picked color's interior bounding-box border.\n\nKey insight:\n- The noisy marker cloud is not preserved as pixels; only the majority marker\n  color and its spatial extent define the output.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"6ca952ad\" lines=\"15-30\">\n<description>\nDrop every large foreground object straight down to the bottom while leaving small marker objects fixed.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has a dominant background color and exactly one foreground color.\n- The foreground is split into separated 4-connected objects.\n- Objects of area at most three are stationary markers; larger objects are moving sprites.\n\nRule:\n- Clear each moving sprite from its original position.\n- Translate each moving sprite vertically downward until its bbox bottom reaches the grid bottom.\n- Repaint each translated sprite in its original color; small markers stay unchanged.\n\nKey insight:\n- Object area separates the active falling sprites from the small fixed markers, and all active sprites follow the same vertical path.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"da515329\" lines=\"11-24\">\n<description>\nGrow four clockwise spiral wall paths from the tips of a teal cross.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = 0 (black). A single teal (8) cross centered in the grid.\n\nRule:\n- Each arm tip launches a \"turtle\" heading 90° right of its outward direction.\n- Movement priority: turn right > go straight > turn left (left only if not on border).\n- Normal step = 2 cells; step right after a turn = 1 cell.\n- Each new cell must have a 1-cell orthogonal gap from all existing walls except the predecessor.\n\nKey insight: The right-turn preference plus alternating 2/1 step lengths creates self-avoiding spirals that tile the grid with uniform spacing.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"2d0172a1\" lines=\"209-224\">\n<description>\nConvert a rough nested line drawing into a clean canonical frame-and-dot diagram.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
